    "about": "The Centers for Habilitation, commonly known as TCH, is an Arizona nonprofit that creates opportunities for individuals with physical and developmental disabilities. The organization provides programs and supports intended to help people live more independent, connected, and fulfilling lives.\n\nTCH serves adults with disabilities through a broad set of support services, including day programs, home and community-based services, group homes, employment services, transportation, and AbilityOne contract work. Its programs are designed to support people in daily living, community participation, skill development, employment readiness, and self-direction.\n\nTCH's day programs combine social, recreational, and educational opportunities so adults with developmental and physical disabilities can broaden their social connections and become more independent citizens. Its home and community-based services support individuals living in their own homes, with family members, or in other community settings by helping with personal care, decision-making, goal planning, budgeting, meals, respite, and community integration.\n\nThe organization also provides group home services in Maricopa and Pima counties and operates accessible transportation services for supported individuals. Its employment services include job readiness, job development, job placement, job retention, supported employment, group supported employment, post-employment assistance, and work adjustment training.\n\nThrough AbilityOne and other enterprise activities, TCH connects people with significant disabilities to employment opportunities. The organization also operates ASDD Document Destruction, an Arizona secure data destruction service and division of TCH.\n\nTCH frames its work around respect, inspiration, adaptability, accountability, and integrity. Across its programs, the organization emphasizes dignity, individual choice, inclusion, health and wellness, self-confidence, and practical support that helps people overcome barriers and improve self-sufficiency.",
